<h1>Reports and Compliance Mappings</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Gate summarizes its own local scan records and attaches maintainer-authored control labels. It does not determine compliance.</p>

<h2 id="report">Local report command</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Reads the current local audit log and writes to the current directory</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report</span>
<span class="dim">gate-report-YYYY-MM-DD.md</span>

<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --format html</span>
<span class="dim">gate-report-YYYY-MM-DD.html</span>

<span class="cmt"># Prints the current JSON transformation to stdout</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate report --format json</span></div>

<p>Markdown and HTML reports are generated from entries in the current <code>~/.gate/audit.jsonl</code>. They are not fresh scans. Rotated generations are not included, there is no report-period flag, and JSON/SARIF scan modes currently return before recording an audit entry.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Do not present a Gate report as an audit, certification, or attestation.</strong> It shows what this version of Gate previously recorded in one local log. Missing entries, skipped files, disabled rules, false negatives, tampered state, rotation, and unsupported secret shapes all limit the evidence.</div>

<h2 id="contents">What Markdown and HTML contain</h2>
<ul>
  <li>generation date;</li>
  <li>finding and severity counts;</li>
  <li>a table of file, line, rule, severity, and stored status;</li>
  <li>the union of static control labels mapped to those rule IDs; and</li>
  <li>generic remediation recommendations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Current data-quality limits</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Repeated detections across separate scans are counted as separate report findings.</li>
  <li>The audit writer does not update old entries after a later fix, rotation, or ignore action. Resolution status is therefore not a reliable workflow ledger.</li>
  <li>The current flattener assigns every finding in an audit entry the first path in that entry's <code>filesScanned</code> array. Multi-file report locations can be wrong.</li>
  <li>Ordinary recorded scans set an entry action of <code>none</code>; that value can appear as the displayed status.</li>
  <li>The report recommendation to rotate credentials flagged live does not mean the report command performed verification.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="frameworks">What the mappings are</h2>
<p>The reporter contains a static table for a subset of rule IDs. A detection may be labelled with references from:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>OWASP Top 10 (2021)</strong>, such as A02, A07, or A08;</li>
  <li><strong>NIST SP 800-53</strong>, such as IA-5, SC-12, or SC-17;</li>
  <li><strong>CIS Controls</strong>, using labels such as CIS 5.2 or CIS 14.4; and</li>
  <li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria</strong>, such as CC6.1, CC6.2, or CC9.2.</li>
</ul>

<p>These are not NIST SP 800-63B assessments or CIS Benchmark v8 checks. They are maintainer-authored associations between secret-pattern rule IDs and broad control themes. Unmapped rules receive no labels. The table does not test control design, operation, evidence quality, compensating controls, scope, or organizational policy.</p>

<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Example detection</th><th>Current labels</th><th>What it does not establish</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>AWS access key ID</td><td>OWASP A02; NIST SC-12/IA-5; CIS 14.4; SOC 2 CC6.1</td><td>That IAM key management is compliant.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GitHub PAT</td><td>OWASP A07; NIST IA-5; SOC 2 CC6.2</td><td>That access scopes, review, or revocation controls operate effectively.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Private key</td><td>OWASP A02; selected NIST/CIS/SOC 2 labels by rule</td><td>That key custody, encryption, rotation, or lifecycle controls are adequate.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unmapped rule</td><td>None</td><td>Anything about compliance or risk.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="audit-log">Audit-log privacy and integrity</h2>
<p>The audit log is hash-chained and can detect some edits when <code>gate audit --verify</code> is run. It is local and recomputable, so it is tamper-evident at best—not append-only or independently witnessed.</p>
<p>The writer masks a finding's <code>match</code> field, but it currently spreads the rest of the finding object unchanged. Scanner findings include a separate <code>secret</code> field, so plaintext credential material can remain in <code>~/.gate/audit.jsonl</code> and in <code>gate audit --export</code>. Inspect and protect that file; do not attach it to tickets or audits without sanitizing it.</p>

<h2 id="machine">Machine formats from scans</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --all --format json</span><span class="dim">   # stdout</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --all --format sarif</span><span class="dim">  # stdout</span></div>
<p>The scan JSON serializer emits redacted matches plus a short secret hash, findings, scan errors, skipped files, and summary counts. SARIF 2.1.0 includes rule metadata and locations; it can include verification properties. Both commands exit non-zero for findings or scan errors, so capture output without assuming a zero exit.</p>
<p><code>gate report --format json</code> is not the same as <code>gate scan --format json</code>. The report path currently feeds audit entries into a serializer designed for scan results, which can yield incomplete or misaligned metadata. Treat that format as experimental.</p>

<h2 id="github">GitHub code scanning</h2>
<p>The GitHub Action can run a second SARIF scan and attempt to upload it when <code>format: sarif</code>. Upload is best-effort and needs an appropriate token, repository permissions, and GitHub code-scanning support. A successful workflow step is not proof that upload or alert ingestion succeeded; read the action log.</p>

<h2 id="responsible">Responsible wording</h2>
<p>A defensible description is: “Gate recorded these pattern findings locally and associated some rule IDs with these control references.” Avoid “Gate proves compliance,” “certified,” “audit-ready,” “complete coverage,” or “all controls passed.”</p>

<div class="info-box"><strong>For real assurance:</strong> pair the raw evidence with a documented scope, independent control testing, reviewer sign-off, retention controls, and another scanner. Treat Gate's mappings as triage hints.</div>
